= Immunotherapy

Allergen immunotherapy is a highly effective long-term treatment that improves symptoms for people with allergic rhinitis (hay fever), stinging insect allergy, allergic asthma, and moderate/severe atopic dermatitis (eczema). By training the body with small amounts of the allergen over the course of years, through either shots or a small tablet under the tongue, we develop tolerance to the allergens - with lasting benefits after therapy finishes, for at least a few years. Many patients find they need much less medication for their allergy symptoms long-term.

Immunotherapy can be considered for patients with allergic conditions who:
Have severe symptoms that interfere with school, work, or other activities
Have symptoms despite avoiding triggers and using medications (i.e. nasal sprays)
No longer wish to use medications or would like a long-lasting treatment

Not all patients can receive immunotherapy; it should be avoided in patients who:
Have uncontrolled asthma or heart disease
Require certain heart medications (beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors)
can't commit to the full duration of therapy

== Timing
- Significant commitment of time: at least 3 years. \<3 years of therapy is associated with symptom improvement that fades quickly after stopping.
- For perennial or year-long allergens such as dust mites, it can be started any time
- In general for seasonal allergens, immunotherapy is started a few months prior to the season to give your body a 'head start' before seeing the real allergen.
- For seasonal allergens, options (usually based on cost and convenience) include:
